  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter a pleasant day today, shortwave trough currently across \nthe mid MS Valley will gradually shift northeast and bring rain\nto much of the region tonight through early Sunday morning. Deep\nmoisture with PWAT's climbing to 1.75-2 inches from the Capital\nRegion south and east by late tonight along with deep warm \ncloud layers will be favorable for efficient warm rain \nprocesses, with locally heavy rainfall possible in these areas. \nSome CAMs also suggest some mid level F-Gen associated north of \nthe main shortwave for areas near the I-90 corridor, possibly \nallowing for bands of moderate to heavy rain to develop farther \nnorth across portions of the Capital Region east into the \nBerkshires and extreme southern VT around or shortly after \ndaybreak Sunday.\n\nLatest HREFs indicate probs for >3\" rainfall in 3 hours at\n10-30% across portions of the mid Hudson Valley/southern\nTaconics and NW CT during the 8 AM-11 AM time period Sunday\nmorning, with probs for >1\" in 3 hours at 70-90% in these same\nareas, and as high as 30-50% as far N/W as Schoharie County, the\ngreater Capital Region northeast into southern VT. Flash Flood\nguidance across much of the area from the Capital Region and\npoints south/east at 2.5-3.5\"/3 hours, so odds generally favor\nlimited potential for flash flooding with mainly ponding of\nwater/nuisance-type flooding of low-lying, poor drainage/urban\nareas. However, isolated flash flooding can not be ruled out \nand therefore WPC has expanded the Marginal Risk for Excessive \nRainfall farther north/west into the SE Catskills, mid Hudson \nValley and NW CT region for late tonight into Sunday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter the morning rainfall, a lull in the precipitation is\nexpected late Sunday morning through early/mid afternoon.\nHowever, as northern stream shortwave and associated cold front\napproach, isolated to scattered showers/thunderstorms are\nexpected to develop late Sunday afternoon across portions of the\nSW Adirondacks and Mohawk Valley before tracking east/southeast\nSunday evening. Mid-level (700-500 hPa) lapse rates steepen to\n6-6.5C/km for areas N/W of the Capital Region late Sunday\nafternoon as mid level cooling/height falls overspread the\nregion from the NW. Although overall coverage is expected to be\nscattered, a few strong/severe thunderstorms may develop late\nSunday afternoon/early evening amid destabilization from mid\nlevel cooling and forcing from approaching height falls/cold\nfront and right entrance to upper level jet translating across\nOntario/SW Quebec Sunday evening. Isolated damaging wind gusts\nand marginally severe hail will be possible, and SPC has placed\nmost of eastern NY and portions of western New England within a\nMarginal Risk for severe thunderstorms. This severe potential\ncould linger for a few hours after sunset before diminishing\nlater Sunday evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nIn the wake of Sunday night's cold front, cooler air will\noverspread the region through early next week, with somewhat\nbreezy conditions expected Monday with west to northwest wind \ngusts of 25-35 mph possible. Highs Monday and Tuesday will only\nreach the 60s for most higher elevations and upper 60s/lower 70s\nin valley areas, with overnight lows mainly in the 40s except\n30s across the southern Adirondacks where some patchy frost is\npossible. Generally fair weather is expected through Wednesday\nbefore unsettled conditions with showers return for\nThursday/Friday as another frontal system approaches from the\nwest.",
